================================================================================
Bundled image assets
================================================================================

Agent marks under public/images/agents/ are redistributed under their own
licenses. Each one's source, license, copyright owner and SHA-256 are recorded
in public/images/agents/asset-rights.json, with the corresponding license text
under licenses/.

  codex.svg        MIT  LobeHub contributors              licenses/lobehub-LICENSE
  opencode.svg     MIT  LobeHub contributors              licenses/lobehub-LICENSE
  claude-code.svg  MIT  LobeHub contributors              licenses/lobehub-LICENSE
  kilo-cli.svg     MIT  LobeHub contributors              licenses/lobehub-LICENSE
  hermes.svg       MIT  LobeHub contributors              licenses/lobehub-LICENSE
  openclaw.svg     MIT  Jason Young (cc-switch contributors)  licenses/cc-switch-LICENSE

hermes.svg is the drawing lobe-icons also publishes as nousresearch.svg,
identical but for its <title>: Hermes Agent is Nous Research's product, so the
company mark identifies it. ChatGPT Desktop renders codex.svg rather than a
second copy of the same file: it is OpenAI's own application and shares Codex's
configuration.

An agent with no entry in asset-rights.json has no bundled mark and renders a
letter glyph instead. That is deliberate: a vendor favicon copied in without an
auditable redistribution basis would be both a licensing and a trademark
problem, so the absence of a mark is the correct state rather than a gap. Aider
and WorkBuddy are in that position -- neither has a mark in lobe-icons, Aider
publishes no brand kit and its Apache-2.0 code licence grants no trademark
rights, and WorkBuddy publishes no vector at all.

BootAgent's own mark under public/images/brand/ and public/favicon.png is
first-party work under this repository's licence, so it carries no third-party
provenance record — recording it in asset-rights.json would claim an origin it
does not have. The mark has been redrawn for the name: a stroked ring with a
round-capped stem set beside it, the b that boots. It is defined as geometry
rather than as artwork -- a circle at cx/cy 66/65 r 33 and a line from 33,8 to
33,98, both 12 units wide, on the viewBox 24 -1 84 108 -- so every raster here
and in MaimoryLab/BootAgent is rendered from that definition rather than traced
from a bitmap. bootagent-logo.png is the 1024px rendering of it and is kept as
the source of record even though nothing on the site references it directly.

